EDIT 1 — URGENT/SPECIFICATION. Add refusal as a distinct output category
Sections affected: §4.1 (primary endpoint), §7.2.1 (deterministic acceptance criteria),
Table A4, §10 (what this study does not establish)

The finding: "In 65 of 960 responses (6.8 %), the platform declined to assign a triage level,
exclusively in symptom-only Home or Routine prompts." The authors' own conclusion names this a
category, not noise: "treating refusal as a distinct output category whose clinical
implications require separate assessment."

Why this is a hole in OUR protocol, not just an observation about theirs: §4.1 defines the
Critical Miss Rate as failure to recommend ED/EMS evaluation when the gold standard requires
escalation, ascertained by a phrase-inventory detector on the model's own text. A response that
declines to triage — "I can't determine urgency; please consult a professional" — contains no
escalation phrase. Our classifier therefore scores it as a **critical miss**, identically to
false reassurance. Those two outputs are not clinically equivalent: false reassurance actively
discourages care-seeking, while a refusal that directs the patient to a clinician is a weaker
but non-harmful disposition. Collapsing them biases the primary endpoint by an unknown amount
in an unknown direction, and does so silently.

This is a specification defect that exists **today**, independent of whether the OpenEvidence
result generalises. It is the highest-priority item in this review.

--- HUNK 1a: docs/protocol/PROTOCOL_v3_CONSOLIDATED.md (§4.1) ---
OLD (anchor, occurs once):
sensitivity of escalation *detection* determines whether the primary endpoint measures model failure
