DELIBERATELY NOT PROPOSED THIS CYCLE

- **Hunter, Ring & Jayakumar, *Inquiry* 2026 (PMID 42665284, doi 10.1177/00469580261485819).**
  Five LLMs on 14 upper-extremity scenarios written below sixth-grade reading level — lay
  register, patient-facing, on our core question. Headline is optimistic (97% PPV / 89% NPV
  initially; 100% PPV / 95% NPV on re-test 24 months later), but the buried finding is ours:
  **compartment syndrome was misidentified as nonurgent in 80% of scenarios on initial inquiry.**
  That is a single-syndrome atypical-catastrophe miss of exactly the class this protocol targets.
  No edit proposed: n = 14 scenarios in one anatomical domain, and the authors' framing (AI could
  "reduce unnecessary use of high-cost emergency services") runs opposite to an undertriage-safety
  reading. It belongs in the library, which it now is. Note the shared senior author with the
  Jaarsma null already cited — worth the PI knowing two of our cited items come from one group.
- **Strechen et al., *Int J Med Inform* 2026 (PMID 42667903)** — VLM triage on physiological
  trend images, 75% concordance with attendings and 7% under-triage versus 14% for physicians in
  training. Inpatient deterioration, not patient-facing disposition; different task. Library only.
- **Christakis & Keren, *JAMA Pediatr* 2026 (PMID 42671856), "Do No Harm This Time—The Urgency of
  AI Chatbot Research."** Editorial in a high-visibility journal whose title states this study's
  thesis. **No abstract is available and the full text was not retrieved**, so nothing is proposed
  and nothing is quoted. Flagged for PI retrieval — an editorial like this is useful in an IRB
  cover letter if its content matches its title.
- **Evaluation-awareness / reward-seeking (PI-raised 3 Sep).** The PI asked whether the Anthropic
  "reward-seeker" result bears on this protocol. The transferable claim is that a model may behave
  differently when a clear grader is detectable — a threat to this benchmark's external validity,
  not to the models under test. Recommended additions were an evaluation-awareness probe and a §10
  limitation naming the Arm A / Arm C grader-salience confound. **Not written as a patch this
  cycle:** the primary source is egress-blocked and only search snippets were obtainable, which is
  below this project's citation bar. Held pending primary retrieval.
